<main id="main">

<section class="gr-hero"><figure><img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1783698906241-cc9069b71d7f?auto=format&fit=crop&w=900&h=760&q=75" alt="cast iron pan cooking steak over glowing coals" width="900" height="760" fetchpriority="high"></figure><div class="gr-hero-t"><p class="gr-k">Flat-top &middot; Skillet &middot; Cast iron</p><h1>Get the pan right and the food takes care of itself.</h1><p>Griddle is an independent guide to cooking on a hot, flat surface. Learn which heat each food needs, keep a cast-iron pan in top condition and scale batter for a crowd.</p><p class="gr-acts"><a class="btn" href="#tools">Open the kitchen tools</a><a class="gr-ln" href="pan-heat-guide.html">How hot is hot?</a></p></div></section>

<section class="gr-heat"><div class="gr-w"><p class="gr-k">The heat scale</p><h2>Four heats every cook uses</h2><div class="gr-bar"><div class="h1"><b>Low</b><span>120&ndash;150&deg;C</span><em>Eggs, sweating onions, warming</em></div><div class="h2"><b>Medium</b><span>150&ndash;190&deg;C</span><em>Pancakes, fish, vegetables</em></div><div class="h3"><b>Medium-high</b><span>190&ndash;220&deg;C</span><em>Burgers, chicken, sandwiches</em></div><div class="h4"><b>High</b><span>220&deg;C and up</span><em>Steak, smash burgers, flatbreads</em></div></div><p class="gr-note">No thermometer? Hold your palm about 5 cm above the pan: you can bear it for about 5 seconds at medium, 3 at medium-high and 1 at high.</p></div></section>

<section class="gr-s" id="tools"><div class="gr-w"><p class="gr-k">Kitchen tools</p><h2>Quick answers before the pan goes on</h2>
<div class="gr-tool"><div class="gr-tool-l"><span>A</span><h3>Pan Temperature Guide</h3><p>Heat, time and the sign it is done.</p><label for="pt-f">I am cooking<select id="pt-f"><option value="pancake">Pancakes</option><option value="egg">Eggs</option><option value="steak">Steak, about 2.5 cm</option><option value="burger">Burgers</option><option value="fish">Fish fillets</option><option value="chicken">Chicken breast, flattened</option><option value="sandwich">Grilled sandwich</option><option value="veg">Vegetables</option><option value="flatbread">Flatbread</option></select></label></div><div class="gr-tool-r" id="pt-o" aria-live="polite"></div></div>
<div class="gr-tool"><div class="gr-tool-l"><span>B</span><h3>Batter Scaler</h3><p>Basic pancake batter for any number of people.</p><label for="bs-p">People<input id="bs-p" type="number" min="1" max="40" value="4" inputmode="numeric"></label><label for="bs-s">Pancakes each<select id="bs-s"><option value="2">2</option><option value="3" selected>3</option><option value="4">4</option></select></label></div><div class="gr-tool-r" id="bs-o" aria-live="polite"></div></div>
<div class="gr-tool"><div class="gr-tool-l"><span>C</span><h3>Seasoning Check</h3><p>Is your cast iron due a fresh coat?</p><label for="sc-l">The surface looks<select id="sc-l"><option value="black">Smooth, even and black</option><option value="patch">Patchy or dull in places</option><option value="rust">Has orange rust spots</option><option value="sticky">Sticky or tacky</option></select></label><label for="sc-u">I cook acidic food in it<select id="sc-u"><option value="0">Rarely</option><option value="1">Often</option></select></label></div><div class="gr-tool-r" id="sc-o" aria-live="polite"></div></div>
</div></section>

<section class="gr-s gr-mus"><div class="gr-w gr-sp"><div><p class="gr-k">Choose your surface</p><h2>Griddle, skillet or cast iron?</h2><p>They all cook with direct contact heat, but they behave differently. Choose by what you cook most.</p></div><div class="tblw"><table class="gr-tbl"><thead><tr><th>Surface</th><th>Heats</th><th>Holds heat</th><th>Best for</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Flat griddle</td><td>Evenly</td><td>Well</td><td>Pancakes, eggs, many burgers at once</td></tr><tr><td>Cast-iron skillet</td><td>Slowly</td><td>Very well</td><td>Steak, cornbread, oven-to-table</td></tr><tr><td>Carbon steel pan</td><td>Fast</td><td>Well</td><td>Stir-fries, searing, crepes</td></tr><tr><td>Non-stick pan</td><td>Fast</td><td>Poorly</td><td>Eggs and fish at medium heat</td></tr><tr><td>Stainless steel</td><td>Fast</td><td>Moderately</td><td>Pan sauces after searing</td></tr></tbody></table></div></div></section>

<section class="gr-s"><div class="gr-w gr-ph"><figure class="gr-ph1"><img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1760192716247-382af2aa9b35?auto=format&fit=crop&w=440&h=440&q=75" alt="two fried eggs sizzling in a black pan" width="440" height="440" loading="lazy"></figure><figure class="gr-ph2"><img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1707335820423-134232cd16be?auto=format&fit=crop&w=440&h=540&q=75" alt="frying pan filled with food on a stove" width="440" height="540" loading="lazy"></figure><div><p class="gr-k">The golden rule</p><h2>Hot pan, cold oil, leave it alone</h2><ol class="gr-rules"><li><b>Preheat the pan</b> for three to five minutes before any food goes in.</li><li><b>Add a thin film of oil</b> just before the food, not long before.</li><li><b>Dry the food</b> with kitchen paper. Water stops browning.</li><li><b>Don&rsquo;t crowd it.</b> Leave space so steam can escape.</li><li><b>Wait.</b> When the crust forms, the food lets go of the pan.</li></ol></div></div></section>

<section class="gr-s gr-dark"><div class="gr-w"><p class="gr-k">In depth</p><h2>The two guides</h2><div class="gr-gds"><a href="pan-heat-guide.html"><img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1763343146204-50aec9ab91ef?auto=format&fit=crop&w=640&h=400&q=75" alt="pieces of seasoned meat cooking on a grill" width="640" height="400" loading="lazy"><div><h3>The Pan Heat Guide</h3><p>Reading heat without a thermometer, smoke points, why food sticks and a timing table for twenty everyday foods.</p><span>Read it</span></div></a><a href="cast-iron-care-guide.html"><img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1789870905199-7d529e41df0a?auto=format&fit=crop&w=640&h=400&q=75" alt="blue speckled enamel skillet on a dark background" width="640" height="400" loading="lazy"><div><h3>The Cast Iron Care Guide</h3><p>Seasoning, cleaning, drying, fixing rust and the foods that build a pan&rsquo;s non-stick surface.</p><span>Read it</span></div></a></div></div></section>

<section class="gr-s"><div class="gr-w gr-oil"><div><p class="gr-k">Fat and heat</p><h2>Smoke points, roughly</h2><p>An oil starts to smoke and taste acrid above its smoke point. Match the fat to the heat.</p></div><ul class="gr-oils"><li><b>Butter</b><i style="width:30%"></i><span>about 150&deg;C</span></li><li><b>Extra-virgin olive oil</b><i style="width:45%"></i><span>about 190&deg;C</span></li><li><b>Ghee</b><i style="width:70%"></i><span>about 250&deg;C</span></li><li><b>Sunflower or rapeseed</b><i style="width:72%"></i><span>about 230&deg;C</span></li><li><b>Groundnut</b><i style="width:75%"></i><span>about 230&deg;C</span></li><li><b>Refined avocado</b><i style="width:90%"></i><span>about 270&deg;C</span></li></ul></div></section>

<section class="gr-s gr-mus"><div class="gr-w gr-fq"><div><p class="gr-k">Asked at the stove</p><h2>Pan questions</h2><figure><img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1710533820700-dd6f6623cc97?auto=format&fit=crop&w=420&h=360&q=75" alt="stack of pancakes with syrup being drizzled" width="420" height="360" loading="lazy"></figure></div><div class="gr-faq"><details open><summary>How do I know when a pan is hot enough?</summary><p>Flick a few drops of water onto the dry pan. If they sizzle and vanish, it is medium. If they bead up and skate across the surface, it is properly hot, around 200&deg;C, which is right for searing.</p></details><details><summary>Why does food stick to my pan?</summary><p>Usually because the pan or the oil was not hot enough, or the food was moved too soon. Most proteins release on their own once a crust has formed. Wait, then lift.</p></details><details><summary>Which oil is best for high-heat cooking?</summary><p>Choose an oil with a high smoke point and a neutral taste, such as sunflower, rapeseed, groundnut or refined avocado oil. Save butter and extra-virgin olive oil for medium heat or for finishing.</p></details><details><summary>Can I wash cast iron with soap?</summary><p>Yes, a little mild washing-up liquid is fine on well-seasoned cast iron. What harms it is soaking, the dishwasher and leaving it wet. Dry it straight away and wipe with a thin film of oil.</p></details><details><summary>Why are my pancakes pale in the middle and burnt outside?</summary><p>The pan is too hot. Pancakes like a steady medium heat, about 190&deg;C on an electric griddle. Cook the first one as a test and adjust before the rest.</p></details><details><summary>How do I stop smoke filling the kitchen?</summary><p>Use less oil, wipe it thin with paper, choose a high smoke point oil and switch on the extractor before you start. Smoking oil means the pan is hotter than the food needs.</p></details></div></div></section>

<section class="gr-nl"><div class="gr-w"><h2>The Griddle Sheet</h2><p>One heat lesson and one pan technique, once a month.</p><a class="btn" href="mailto:hello@griddle.com?subject=The%20Griddle%20Sheet&amp;body=Please%20subscribe%20me.">Subscribe by email</a></div></section>

